## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design multi-tenant prompt systems, policy-driven routing, and drift auditing; checks comfort with latency budgeting and audit trails; reveals understanding of safety constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant policy blobs
- Model routing via data_classifications
- Safety guard injection preserving intent
- Drift detection and latency budgeting
- Audit log design and replayability

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal prototype illustrating routing and rewriting
function routePrompt(prompt, tenantPolicy, models) {
  // decide avatar
  const avatar = tenantPolicy.data_classifications.includes('medical') ? models.avatarA : models.avatarB;
  // rewrite to inject guard
  const guarded = `Guarded: ${prompt} [guard:${tenantPolicy.allowed_actions.join(',')}]`;
  // fake latency
  return { guarded, avatar: avatar.name };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale the audit log and ensure tamper-evidence across distributed systems?
- How would you test reliability against prompt paraphrasing or adversarial prompts?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a practical, risk-aware prompt orchestration layer for high-sensitivity contexts, with concrete routing logic and fail-safes.

## Key Concepts

- Prompt orchestration and modular routing
- Signal fusion (intent, sensitivity, latency)
- Safety-first edge cases and auditing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal prototype sketch
function routePrompt(intent, sensitivity, latencyMs) {
  // simple scoring example
  const modules = {
    'SQL-builder': {score: 0, safe: latencyMs > 200},
    'Privacy-masker': {score: 2, safe: true},
    'Result-summarizer': {score: latencyMs <= 500, safe: true}
  };
  // bias on conflicts toward Privacy-masker
  const pick = latencyMs < 1000 ? 'Privacy-masker' : 'SQL-builder';
  return {module: pick, payload: {prompt: 'assembled', constraints: {maskPII: true}}};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you unit-test edge cases like conflicting signals or missing budget?
- What metrics would you collect to detect routing misclassifications or latency violations?

## Why This Is Asked
This probes system design for multi-tenant prompt governance with decomposition, guardrails, and auditability.

## Key Concepts
- Prompt decomposition
- Tenant-specific policy enforcement
- Guardrail injection and leakage prevention
- Provenance and audit trails

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal prototype illustrating flow
function governanceFlow(request, policy){
  const subs = decompose(request);
  const guarded = subs.map(s => injectGuard(s, policy));
  const finalOut = assemble(guarded);
  const audit = {tenant: policy.tenant, ts: Date.now(), steps: subs.length};
  return {finalOut, audit};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate guardrails against adversarial prompts and policy drift?
- What metrics would you instrument to measure governance efficacy and latency?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design scalable, privacy-aware prompt routing with safety checks under latency constraints, a common challenge in enterprise LLM deployments.

## Key Concepts

- Tenant-aware routing
- Privacy-preserving sanitization
- Latency-budget guided model selection
- Guardrails and veto logic
- Pluggable sanitizer architecture

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal gating prototype (JS)
class Gate {
  constructor() {}
  route(prompt, tenant) {
    const risk = {
      privacy: tenant.privacyRisk || 0,
      safety: tenant.safetyRisk || 0,
      latency: tenant.latencyBudget || 100
    };
    const model = risk.latency < 60 ? 'lite' : 'standard';
    const template = risk.privacy > 7 || risk.safety > 7 ? 'guarded' : 'default';
    return { tenant: tenant.id, model, template };
  }
}
const g = new Gate();
console.log(g.route('Prompt', {id: 'tenantA', privacyRisk:8, safetyRisk:5, latencyBudget:40}));
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test guardrails under adversarial prompts?
- How would you scale to thousands of tenants with dynamic policies?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to extend routing with governance constraints and to design testable prototypes.

## Key Concepts
- multi-module routing with governance
- signal design: intent, data_sensitivity, governance_policy
- edge-case handling: conflicts, missing role

## Code Example
```javascript
function route(prompt, signals){
  if (signals.governance_policy === 'restrict') return {module:'Data-Governance', payload:{prompt}};
  if (signals.data_sensitivity === 'PII') return {module:'PII-redactor', payload:{prompt}};
  return {module:'SQL-builder', payload:{prompt}};
}
module.exports = route;
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you unit test the routing logic for edge cases like conflicting signals?
- How would you extend the router to support additional governance policies without breaking existing behavior?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests lifecycle design for prompt templates, including versioning, canary deployments, per-tenant experiments, and safe rollback, all critical in production chat systems for high-safety domains.

## Key Concepts
- Versioned, tenant-scoped templates
- Canary rollouts and drift monitoring
- Safe rollback via veto gates and audit logs
- Data model: Tenant, TemplateVersion, Deployment, Experiment, RollbackLog

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal prototype: tenant -> version
const registry = {
  'tenantA': { current: 'v2', canary: 'v3' },
};

function resolveLatestVersion(tenant, canary = false) {
  const t = registry[tenant] || { current: null };
  return canary && t.canary ? t.canary : t.current;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you measure drift in template performance and safety?
- How would you implement per-tenant experiments without latency penalties?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests architecture for intent resolution, safe routing, and token budgeting under real-world noise.

## Key Concepts
- Intent detection and confidence thresholds
- Template routing to data-extraction, summarization, code-generation
- Safety guards to prevent system-prompt leakage
- Token budgeting and edge-case handling

## Code Example
```typescript
type Template = 'data-extraction'|'summarization'|'code-generation';
type Payload = { template: Template; tokenBudget: number; justification: string; };
function routePrompt(prompt: string, signals: {intent?: string; confidence?: number;}): Payload {
  const t = (signals.intent === 'summarize' && (signals.confidence ?? 0) > 0.8) ? 'summarization'
            : (signals.intent === 'extract' && (signals.confidence ?? 0) > 0.8) ? 'data-extraction'
            : (signals.intent === 'generate' && (signals.confidence ?? 0) > 0.8) ? 'code-generation'
            : 'summarization';
  const budgets: Record<Template, number> = {'data-extraction': 800, 'summarization': 500, 'code-generation': 1200};
  return { template: t, tokenBudget: budgets[t], justification: 'inferred from signals with fallback' };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to n templates and multilingual prompts?
- How would you test guardrail effectiveness under prompt injections?
